+
+---
+
+## P0011 — MMTM register-list mask is REVERSED vs MMFM (was mapped identically)
+
+**Status:** ✅ APPLIED + VERIFIED 2026-07-05. `grep -rn P0011 rtl/`.
+
+**Applied edits (all in the vendored core):**
+- `rtl/core/tms34010_core.sv` (mm iterator): the priority encoder now selects the
+  **highest** set mask bit for **both** MMTM and MMFM (was: MMFM highest / MMTM
+  lowest); new signal `mm_reg_idx = is_mmtm ? (4'd15 - mm_iter_idx) : mm_iter_idx`
+  drives read-port-1 for the MMTM push (was `mm_iter_idx`). The bit-clear + address
+  stepping keep using `mm_iter_idx` (the bit index).
+- `rtl/core/tms34010_decode.sv`: corrected the MMTM/MMFM A0026 comments.
+- `sim/tb/tb_mmtm.sv`, `sim/tb/tb_mmfm.sv`, `sim/tb/tb_mmtm_nflag.sv`: masks updated to
+  the correct reversed-MMTM encoding (bit-reverse of the direct-map word), and a
+  **reset-vector init** added (see "Harness" below).
+
+**Problem.** MMTM and MMFM use **opposite** register-list bit orderings:
+- **MMFM**: mask bit N = register R(N)      (direct).
+- **MMTM**: mask bit N = register R(15−N)   (reversed).
+
+So the TI assembler emits **mirror-image** list words for a matching push/pop pair.
+birdybro (assumption A0026) mapped **both** as `bit N = R(N)`, which passes its own
+round-trip TB (same word for push and pop → self-consistent) but is wrong on real
+code, where a routine pushes and pops the **same** registers with **different**
+(reversed) words. Result: MMTM pushed the mirror-image register set, so a later MMFM
+popped garbage.
+
+**Evidence (real ROM + MAME, authoritative).**
+- **MAME `34010ops.hxx`:** `MMFM` loops `for(i=15;i>=0;i--){ if(l&0x8000) R(i)=RLONG(Rp); Rp+=0x20; l<<=1; }`
+  → bit N controls R(N). `MMTM` loops `for(i=0;i<16;i++){ if(l&0x8000){ Rp-=0x20; WLONG(Rp,R(i)); } l<<=1; }`
+  → bit (15−N) controls R(N), i.e. bit N = R(15−N).
+- **`unidasm -arch tms34010` on Smash T.V.:** matching pairs carry bit-reversed words —
+  `MMTM SP,A0,A7` = `0x8100` vs `MMFM SP,A0,A7` = `0x0081`; `MMTM SP,A0,A4,A6,A7`
+  (`ffea8df0`) = `0x8b00` vs `MMFM SP,A0,A4,A6,A7` (`ffea8f70`) = `0x00d1`. `0x8b00`
+  is the exact bit-reverse of `0x00d1`.
+- **The failure that surfaced it:** the boot's string routine at `ffea8df0` does
+  `MMTM SP,A0,A4,A6,A7` (`0x8b00`) … `MMFM SP,A0,A4,A6,A7` (`0x00d1`). Pre-fix the core
+  read `0x8b00` as regs {A8,A9,A11,A15} and pushed those, so the MMFM popped a code
+  address into **A0** (measured `A0<=0xFFEA57B0` at the `ffea8f90` pop, `tb_yunit_derail`).
+  That made `ffea9090: JRLS` (after `CMP A10,A0`) fall through where MAME takes it →
+  derail to an illegal opcode at `fff0a78a` (cyc 1745583). Post-fix `A0` is the correct
+  small count, the JRLS is **taken** (matches MAME), and the boot runs with **no derail
+  through 6M cycles** (was: derail at 1.75M).
+
+**Why boot appeared to "work" before:** the code reaching `FFE0F8A0` used mostly
+contiguous masks and/or didn't let a mis-popped register reach a compared value, so
+all 31 regs still matched MAME at main entry (identical pre/post fix). The non-
+contiguous `{A0,A4,A6,A7}` pair in the string routine was the first to route a
+mirror-swapped register into a control-flow compare.
+
+**Harness note (pre-existing, fixed here).** Our P0001 reset-vector read means the
+core now fetches its initial PC from `0xFFFFFFE0`. The MM unit TBs never initialized
+that vector; in their small NOP-filled memories it wrapped to a NOP word, so the
+initial PC came up `0x03000300` and (for `tb_mmtm`) skipped the register-load
+preamble — a **pre-existing** breakage independent of P0011 (`tb_mmtm` failed
+identically on the un-patched core). Fixed by pointing the reset vector at word 0
+(`mem[510]=mem[511]=0` in the 512-word TBs; `tb_mmfm`'s vector words were already 0).
+
+**Verification.** `tb_mmtm` (absolute stack layout, 8 regs), `tb_mmfm` (TI-spec
+example + round-trip), `tb_mmtm_nflag` all **PASS** under Questa FSE 25.1std; the
+integrated boot (`tb_yunit_derail`) runs past the old derail.
+
+**Upstream note.** Real core bug, not Y-unit-specific — offer upstream to birdybro
+together with the reversed-MMTM unit vectors (birdybro's round-trip TB used the same
+word for push and pop, the exact blind spot that hid this — same family as P0003/P0008).
diff --git a/rtl/core/tms34010_core.sv b/rtl/core/tms34010_core.sv
index 04bd3ad..5f4abe2 100644
--- a/rtl/core/tms34010_core.sv
+++ b/rtl/core/tms34010_core.sv
@@ -984,15 +984,23 @@ module tms34010_core
   //     final value (= initial + 32*count) points one word past the data,
   //     written back as the new Rp.
   //
-  // Assumption A0026: bit N of the mask = register R(N) for both
-  // instructions. The spec's chart was a graphical figure unrecoverable
-  // from pdftotext; the MMTM→MMFM round-trip test is the real check, and
-  // it only depends on MMTM/MMFM agreeing on the mapping, not its
-  // absolute value.
+  // Bit->register mapping (P0011, was assumption A0026): MMTM and MMFM use
+  // OPPOSITE mask bit orderings — verified against MAME's decoder (unidasm)
+  // on real Smash T.V. code, where a matching push/pop pair carries
+  // mirror-image list words (e.g. MMTM 0x8B00 / MMFM 0x00D1 both name
+  // {A0,A4,A6,A7}):
+  //   - MMFM: mask bit N = register R(N)      (direct;  SPVU001A p.12-109).
+  //   - MMTM: mask bit N = register R(15-N)   (reversed; SPVU001A p.12-111).
+  // The earlier "bit N = R(N) for both" (A0026) passed birdybro's
+  // round-trip TB (it only checks push-then-pop with the SAME word) but is
+  // wrong on real code, which pushes and pops the same registers with
+  // DIFFERENT (reversed) words. mm_iter_idx below is the selected MASK BIT;
+  // mm_reg_idx is the register it names (complemented for MMTM).
   // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   logic [DATA_WIDTH-1:0] mm_rp_q;
   logic [15:0]           mm_mask_q;
   logic [3:0]            mm_iter_idx;
+  logic [3:0]            mm_reg_idx;
   logic                  mm_mask_will_be_empty;
   logic                  is_mmtm, is_mmfm, is_mm;
 
@@ -1000,22 +1008,22 @@ module tms34010_core
   assign is_mmfm = (decoded.iclass == INSTR_MMFM);
   assign is_mm   = is_mmtm || is_mmfm;
 
+  // Both MMTM and MMFM process the HIGHEST set mask bit first, one bit per
+  // memory transaction, clearing it after each ack (P0011). Highest-first is
+  // correct for both because their register maps are mirror images:
+  //   - MMFM (bit N = R(N)):    bit 15 = highest register, restored first
+  //     from the lowest stack address (postincrement).  MAME i=15..0.
+  //   - MMTM (bit N = R(15-N)): bit 15 = register R0, pushed first to the
+  //     highest stack address (predecrement).            MAME i=0..15.
   always_comb begin
     mm_iter_idx = 4'd0;
-    if (is_mmfm) begin
-      // MMFM: highest set bit. Iterate low→high so the LAST overwrite
-      // (the largest i with mm_mask_q[i]=1) wins.
-      for (int i = 0; i < 16; i++) begin
-        if (mm_mask_q[i]) mm_iter_idx = 4'(i);
-      end
-    end else begin
-      // MMTM: lowest set bit. Iterate high→low so the loop terminates
-      // on the smallest i with mm_mask_q[i]=1.
-      for (int i = 15; i >= 0; i--) begin
-        if (mm_mask_q[i]) mm_iter_idx = 4'(i);
-      end
+    for (int i = 0; i < 16; i++) begin
+      if (mm_mask_q[i]) mm_iter_idx = 4'(i);   // last (highest) set bit wins
     end
   end
+  // Register named by the current mask bit: direct for MMFM, 15's-complement
+  // for MMTM (the reversed map). Drives read port 1 for the MMTM push.
+  assign mm_reg_idx = is_mmtm ? (4'd15 - mm_iter_idx) : mm_iter_idx;
   // After we clear the bit at mm_iter_idx, will the mask be empty? Gates
   // the FSM transition (last transaction → WRITEBACK) and, for MMTM,
   // suppresses the final Rp-decrement.
@@ -1177,7 +1185,7 @@ module tms34010_core
                      : is_pblt ? ((state_q == CORE_PBLT_SETUP_WIN) ? CPW_WSTART_IDX
                                 : (state_q == CORE_PBLT_SETUP2) ? B_COLOR0_IDX
                                 : (state_q == CORE_PBLT_SETUP)  ? B_SPTCH_IDX : B_SADDR_IDX)
-                     : (state_q == CORE_MEMORY && is_mmtm) ? mm_iter_idx
+                     : (state_q == CORE_MEMORY && is_mmtm) ? mm_reg_idx
                      : (is_drav && (state_q == CORE_DRAV_SETUP_WIN)) ? CPW_WSTART_IDX // WSTART
                      : (is_drav && (state_q == CORE_DRAV)) ? B_COLOR1_IDX  // COLOR1 for the draw
                      : (is_line && (state_q == CORE_LINE_SETUP1)) ? B_SADDR_IDX   // d (B0)
diff --git a/rtl/core/tms34010_decode.sv b/rtl/core/tms34010_decode.sv
index 970e0ac..b30ffa5 100644
--- a/rtl/core/tms34010_decode.sv
+++ b/rtl/core/tms34010_decode.sv
@@ -287,23 +287,26 @@ module tms34010_decode
   // page 12-111. Encoding `0000 1001 100R DDDD` (top11 =
   // 11'b00001001_100), where R=instr[4] is the register file and
   // DDDD=instr[3:0] is Rp's index. The second instruction word is a
-  // 16-bit binary mask indicating which registers are in the list
-  // (assumption A0026: bit N of mask = register R(N) for both MMTM
-  // and MMFM). Iteration order is lowest-order register first per
-  // the spec's "lowest order register is always saved first".
-  // For each set bit, Rp is predecremented by 32 and the matching
-  // register's 32-bit value is written. Final Rp is left pointing
-  // at the lowest written address.
+  // 16-bit binary mask indicating which registers are in the list.
+  // P0011 (corrected A0026): for MMTM the map is REVERSED —
+  // mask bit N = register R(15-N) — so the lowest-numbered register
+  // (highest mask bit) is saved first per the spec's "lowest order
+  // register is always saved first". For each register, in ascending
+  // register order, Rp is predecremented by 32 and the 32-bit value is
+  // written; final Rp points at the lowest written address. The
+  // iterator + reversed map live in tms34010_core.sv (mm_reg_idx).
   localparam logic [10:0] MMTM_TOP11      = 11'b0000_1001_100;
 
   // MMFM Rp, register list — Move Multiple from Memory (the pop
   // counterpart of MMTM). Per SPVU001A page 12-109. Encoding
   // `0000 1001 101R DDDD` (top11 = 11'b00001001_101), with R=instr[4]
   // the register file and DDDD=instr[3:0] the Rp index. The second
-  // instruction word is the same 16-bit register-list mask used by
-  // MMTM (A0026: bit N = register R(N)). Iteration order is
-  // HIGHEST-order register first per the spec ("the highest order
-  // register is always restored first"). For each set bit, a 32-bit
+  // instruction word is a 16-bit register-list mask. For MMFM the map
+  // is DIRECT — mask bit N = register R(N) — the mirror image of MMTM's
+  // reversed map (P0011), so the assembler emits different list words
+  // for a matching MMFM/MMTM pair. Iteration order is HIGHEST-order
+  // register first per the spec ("the highest order register is always
+  // restored first"). For each set bit, a 32-bit
   // value is read from mem[Rp] into the register and Rp is then
   // post-incremented by 32 — including after the last register, so
   // final Rp = initial Rp + 32*count (points one word past the data).
